,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**Short answer:** Yes, in my understanding. The Central Bank of Ireland permits a UCITS ETF to have both listed (ETF) and unlisted share classes in the same fund. I can't confirm the exact current wording or numbering of Q&A 1088, so check the latest CBI UCITS Q&A document.\n\n**Disclosure the prospectus should make clear (comparison of the classes):**\n- **Dealing:** ETF classes deal in the primary market through authorised participants, and other investors trade on exchange. Unlisted classes subscribe and redeem directly with the fund.\n- **Settlement:** exchange and CSD settlement cycles versus the transfer-agent settlement timetable.\n- **Costs:** TER, brokerage, bid-ask spreads and any duties or anti-dilution levies, which may differ.\n- **Minimums:** creation-unit sizes versus minimum initial and holding amounts.\n- **Rights:** any differences in redemption access, including direct redemption when the secondary market is disrupted.\n- **Liquidity:** depth of the exchange market versus fund-dealing frequency.\n- **Price:** exchange price, which can sit at a premium or discount to NAV, versus dealing at NAV.\n\nThe ESMA ETF guidelines (2014/937) still apply, including the \"UCITS ETF\" identifier.","ttft_ms":5903,"type":"result","duration_ms":10080,"uuid":"631be096-c988-4851-8ae8-8004734c0bb3","ttft_stream_ms":1475,"time_to_request_ms":50,"first_content_frame_ms":1475,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
